// Questions and tables (added 2 Nov 2026, profile 1.26, as an upgrade pass that leaves the
// argument as it was). The essay weighs no rival accounts: the objection that belief cannot be
// willed is met as an objection and answered, not weighed as a rival account of how belief is
// formed, so no `account` frame is declared. Eight claims are new, all quotations: the seven under
// the definition of a genuine option, which carry James's own illustrations, and the empiricists'
// way of believing in truth, which the closing conditional relies on.
//
// The mechanism (added 2 Nov 2026, the same day). Ported from the parallel-method trial of
// 30 Sep 2026 and checked again against the text, but added as the series pass adds one: the
// argument is as it was, and the 30 claims that state a step it does not use stand unwired in the
// sections where James uses them. Five chains: what produces our beliefs, faith that creates its
// fact, what believing and waiting risk, the religious hypothesis supposed true, and tolerance.
// The kinds are James's own groupings. Passion: the duties about truth and error are "only
// expressions of our passional life" (p. 18), the logicians' law rests on "their own natural wish"
// (p. 11), the veto is "intellect with one passion laying down its law" (p. 27). Holding back: each
// case is likened to waiting for evidence ("It is like a general", p. 19; "It is as if a man
// should hesitate", p. 26; "just as a man who in a company of gentlemen made no advances", p. 28),
// and what each forgoes is offered as what truth, religion's good and the gods' acquaintance are
// like.

// Form of the top-level move: a refutation of the evidentialist veto (Clifford) by showing
// that suspension of belief is itself a passional, risk-bearing option, combined with an
// elimination of the veto's possible warrants (objective certitude fails; the preference
// for avoiding error over gaining truth is itself a passion; a rule that would block real
// truths is irrational). The thesis of section IV is then applied to the religious
// hypothesis, and a closing conclusion about mutual tolerance is drawn from it.

# The contentions {isGroup: true}

[Passional nature must decide genuine options]: "Our passional nature not only lawfully may, but must, decide an option between propositions, whenever it is a genuine option that cannot by its nature be decided on intellectual grounds" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 11", note: "James's own statement of 'the thesis I defend', section IV. The thesis is restricted to genuine options: forced, living and momentous.", question: {id: q2, move: answers}}
    <+ <Suspension is itself a passional decision>
    <+ <The evidentialist rule is itself a passion>
    <+ <Believe truth and shun error are separable laws>
    <+ <No objective certitude>
    <+ <Faith can create its own facts>
    <+ <Moral questions cannot wait for proof>
    <- [Belief cannot be willed]
    <- [Clifford's rule]
    +> [The agnostic veto is irrational]

[The agnostic veto is irrational]: James "cannot see my way to accepting the agnostic rules for truth-seeking, or wilfully agree to keep my willing nature out of the game", because "a rule of thinking which would absolutely prevent me from acknowledging certain kinds of truth if those kinds of truth were really there, would be an irrational rule". {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 28", note: "The essay's headline application: to the religious hypothesis. Restated in abstracto at p. 29 as 'we have the right to believe at our own risk any hypothesis that is live enough to tempt our will'."}
    <+ <Religion is a genuine option>
    <+ <Scepticism about religion is itself a risky option>
    <+ <A veto that could block real truth is irrational>
    <- [Self-creating cases are childish and irrelevant to religion]
    <- [The freedom to believe licenses patent superstition]
    +> [Respect one another's mental freedom]

[Respect one another's mental freedom]: "No one of us ought to issue vetoes to the other, nor should we bandy words of abuse. We ought, on the contrary, delicately and profoundly to respect one another's mental freedom" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 30", note: "The closing practical conclusion: 'then only shall we bring about the intellectual republic'."}
    <+ [Waiting is also acting at our peril]: "Indeed we may wait if we will,---I hope you do not think that I am denying that,---but if we do so, we do so at our peril as much as if we believed. In either case we act, taking our life in our hands." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 30", about: [{stance: wait, acts: yes}, {stance: believe, acts: yes}], causes: {from: suspend, to: truth, sign: "-", on: chance, basis: asserted, chain: risk}}

[Only mutual respect brings the intellectual republic]: We ought "to respect one another's mental freedom: then only shall we bring about the intellectual republic" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 30", causes: {from: respect, to: republic, sign: "+", necessary: true, basis: asserted, chain: republic}}

# Preliminary distinctions (section I) {isGroup: true}

[Live hypothesis]: "A live hypothesis is one which appeals as a real possibility to him to whom it is proposed." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 2", note: "Liveness is not intrinsic but a relation to the individual thinker, measured by willingness to act."}

[Genuine option]: "we may call an option a genuine option when it is of the forced, living, and momentous kind." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 3", note: "An option is 'the decision between two hypotheses'. Living: both hypotheses live. Forced: 'there is no standing place outside of the alternative'. Momentous: the opportunity is unique, the stake significant, or the decision irreversible."}

// James's illustrations of the three distinctions: each places one imagined offer on one of them.
[Genuine option]
    <+ [Theosophist or Mohammedan is probably a dead option]: "it is probably a dead option, because for you neither hypothesis is likely to be alive"
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 3", about: {option: theosophist, living: dead}, note: "Of the offer 'Be a theosophist or be a Mohammedan'. 'Probably' is James's hedge and stays in the claim."}
    <+ [Agnostic or Christian is a living option]: "trained as you are, each hypothesis makes some appeal, however small, to your belief"
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 3", about: {option: agnostic, living: living}, note: "Of the offer 'Be an agnostic or be a Christian': 'it is otherwise'."}
    <+ [The umbrella option is not genuine, for it is not forced]: "I do not offer you a genuine option, for it is not forced. You can easily avoid it by not going out at all."
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 3", about: {option: umbrella, forced: avoidable, genuine: no}, note: "Of the offer 'Choose between going out with your umbrella or without it'. The one row whose verdict James states, and the one the definition's rule is seen to work on."}
    <+ [Loving or hating, and judging a theory, are avoidable]: "your option is avoidable. You may remain indifferent to me, neither loving nor hating, and you may decline to offer any judgment as to my theory."
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 3", about: [{option: love, forced: avoidable}, {option: theory, forced: avoidable}]}
    <+ [Accepting a truth or going without it is forced]: "I put on you a forced option, for there is no standing place outside of the alternative"
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 3", about: {option: accept, forced: forced}, note: "Of the offer 'Either accept this truth or go without it'. 'Every dilemma based on a complete logical disjunction, with no possibility of not choosing, is an option of this forced kind.'"}
    <+ [Nansen's expedition would be a momentous option]: "if I were Dr. Nansen and proposed to you to join my North Pole expedition, your option would be momentous"
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 4", about: {option: nansen, momentous: momentous}, note: "Momentous because the opportunity is unique: 'He who refuses to embrace a unique opportunity loses the prize as surely as if he tried and failed.'"}
    <+ [The chemist's option is trivial]: "Such trivial options abound in the scientific life. A chemist finds an hypothesis live enough to spend a year in its verification: he believes in it to that extent. But if his experiments prove inconclusive either way, he is quit for his loss of time, no vital harm being done."
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 4", about: {option: chemist, momentous: trivial}}

[Refusing a unique opportunity loses the prize]: "He who refuses to embrace a unique opportunity loses the prize as surely as if he tried and failed." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 3", causes: {from: refuse, to: prize, sign: "-", basis: asserted, scope: general, chain: risk}}

# The objection from the psychology of belief (section II) {isGroup: true}

[Belief cannot be willed]: "Does it not seem preposterous on the very face of it to talk of our opinions being modifiable at will?" We cannot by willing it believe that Lincoln's existence is a myth or that two one-dollar bills sum to a hundred: "We can say any of these things, but we are absolutely impotent to believe them" #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 4-5", note: "The first of the two views James canvasses on the psychology of opinion; Pascal's wager is offered as the example that makes willed belief look 'silly'. James concedes the point for dead hypotheses only.", causes: {from: willing, to: belief, sign: "0", basis: example, chain: psychology, given: ["dead hypotheses"], stance: rejected}}
    <- [Only dead hypotheses are beyond the will]: "It is only our already dead hypotheses that our willing nature is unable to bring to life again But what has made them dead for us is for the most part" a previous action of our willing nature of an antagonistic kind. {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "pp. 8-9", note: "The source lacks a full stop after 'again'; quoted as printed.", causes: [{from: willing, to: live, sign: "-", basis: asserted, chain: psychology, given: ["a previous action of our willing nature of an antagonistic kind"]}, {from: willing, to: live, sign: "0", basis: asserted, chain: psychology, given: ["hypotheses already dead"]}]}
    <+ [Pascal's wager is not a living option]: "It is evident that unless there be some pre-existing tendency to believe in masses and holy water, the option offered to the will by Pascal is not a living option." #conceded {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 6", note: "James grants that a faith adopted by mechanical calculation 'would lack the inner soul of faith's reality'; the concession is later reversed for cases where 'the previous passional work has been already in their own direction' (p. 11).", about: {option: pascal, living: dead}, causes: {from: pretend, to: live, sign: "+", necessary: true, basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}

[Pascal: take holy water, and belief will come]: "Go, then, and take holy water, and have masses said; belief will come and stupefy your scruples" #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 6", note: "Pascal's wager, in James's free translation.", causes: {from: pascal, to: belief, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: psychology, stance: rejected}}

# Clifford's evidentialism and the scientific ethos (section II) {isGroup: true}

[Clifford's rule]: "It is wrong always, everywhere, and for every one, to believe anything upon insufficient evidence." #reported {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 8", note: "Quoted by James from Clifford, 'The Ethics of Belief'. The position the essay is written against."}
    <+ <The scientific ethos>

<The scientific ethos>: The physical sciences were reared by "patience and postponement", "choking down of preference" and "submission to the icy laws of outer fact", so that "every little sentimentalist who comes blowing his voluntary smoke-wreaths" seems "besotted and contemptible" and the loyalties bred in science "go dead against its toleration". #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 7", note: "James sets this out as the second, 'vile' aspect of believing by volition, and admits 'All this strikes one as healthy' (p. 8). The map draws it as the ground scientists give for Clifford's rule."}
    <_ <Science's options are trivial, so its ethos does not generalise>

<Science's options are trivial, so its ethos does not generalise>: In science the sceptical attitude is right because its options are not genuine; that gives no rule for forced, momentous, living options. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 19-22"}

(1) [Scientific options are not genuine]: In our dealings with objective nature "The questions here are always trivial options, the hypotheses are hardly living (at any rate not living for us spectators), the choice between believing truth or falsehood is seldom forced." {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 20", about: {option: science, momentous: trivial}}
(2) [Sceptical balance is wise where no option is forced]: "The attitude of sceptical balance is therefore the absolutely wise one if we would escape mistakes." #conceded {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 20", note: "James's own scope concession: 'wherever there is no forced option, the dispassionately judicial intellect with no pet hypothesis... ought to be our ideal' (pp. 21-22).", causes: {from: suspend, to: error, sign: "-", basis: asserted, chain: risk, given: ["wherever there is no forced option"]}}
(3) [Truth is unlikely to be adjusted to our needs]: "It seems a priori improbable that the truth should be so nicely adjusted to our needs and powers as that." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 22", note: "'That' is the supposition that we can 'always wait with impunity till the coercive evidence shall have arrived'."}
-----
(4) [Forced speculative options may exist]: James asks, "Are there not somewhere forced options in our speculative questions, and can we (as men who may be interested at least as much in positively gaining truth as in merely escaping dupery) always wait with impunity till the coercive evidence shall have arrived?" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 22", note: "A rhetorical question; read as asserting that there may be forced speculative options for which the scientific rule of waiting gives no guidance, which is what section IX then argues."}

[Clifford: such belief spreads like a pestilence]: Clifford's duty "is to guard ourselves from such beliefs as from a pestilence which may shortly master our own body and then spread to the rest of the town" #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 8", causes: {from: badbelief, to: town, sign: "+", hedged: true, basis: asserted, chain: risk, stance: rejected}}

[Passionate desires advanced science]: "science would be far less advanced than she is if the passionate desires of individuals to get their own faiths confirmed had been kept out of the game." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 21", causes: {from: willing, to: sciadv, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}

[The useful investigator is interested and nervous]: "The most useful investigator, because the most sensitive observer, is always he whose eager interest in one side of the question is balanced by an equally keen nervousness lest he become deceived." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 21", causes: {from: interest, to: observer, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}

[Physical facts are independent of us]: "Throughout the breadth of physical nature facts are what they are quite independently of us" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 20", causes: {from: belief, to: nature, sign: "0", basis: asserted, scope: general, chain: creation}}

[Science's loyalties go against believing by volition]: "The whole system of loyalties which grow up in the schools of science go dead against its toleration" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 7", note: "'Its' is believing by our volition.", causes: {from: loyalties, to: intolerance, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}

# Passion already shapes belief (section III) {isGroup: true}

<The evidentialist rule is itself a passion>: Our non-intellectual nature does in fact influence our convictions, and the logicians' rule that would exclude it is itself only their wish. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 8-11"}

(1) [Willing nature makes hypotheses live or dead]: By 'willing nature' James means "all such factors of belief as fear and hope, prejudice and passion, imitation and partisanship, the circumpressure of our caste and set." {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 9"}
(2) [Our beliefs rest on prestige, not insight]: "not insight, but the prestige of the opinions, is what makes the spark shoot from them and light up our sleeping magazines of faith." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 9", causes: {from: prestige, to: belief, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}
(3) [Belief in truth itself is a volition]: Our belief that there is a truth and that our minds are made for it is "a passionate affirmation of desire, in which our social system backs us up"; against the pyrrhonist "It is just one volition against another". {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 9-10"}
(4) [We disbelieve what we have no use for]: "As a rule we disbelieve all facts and theories for which we have no use." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 10", note: "Illustrated by Clifford, Huxley, Newman and the scientists who will not look at the evidence for telepathy.", causes: {from: use, to: belief, sign: "+", basis: example, scope: general, chain: psychology}}
-- {uses: [1, 2, 3, 4]} --
(5) [Our non-intellectual nature does influence our convictions]: "Evidently, then, our non-intellectual nature does influence our convictions." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 11", question: {id: q1, move: answers}, causes: {from: willing, to: belief, sign: "+", basis: example, scope: general, chain: psychology}}
-- {uses: [4, 5]} --
(6) [The logicians' rule is itself a wish]: "This very law which the logicians would impose upon us" "is based on nothing but their own natural wish to exclude all elements for" which they, as logicians, can find no use. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 10-11", causes: {from: wish, to: lrule, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}

[Authority makes hypotheses live or dead]: Mr. Balfour "gives the name of 'authority' to all those influences, born of the intellectual climate, that make hypotheses possible or impossible for us, alive or dead." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 9", causes: {from: authority, to: live, sign: which, basis: testimony, chain: psychology}}

[Later passions are too late unless earlier ones prepared the way]: "There are passional tendencies and volitions which run before and others which come after belief, and it is only the latter that are too late for the fair; and they are not too late when the previous passional work has been already in their own direction." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 11", causes: {from: willing, to: belief, sign: "0", basis: asserted, chain: psychology, given: ["volitions that come after belief, unless the previous passional work ran in their direction"]}}

[Pascal's argument completes a prepared faith]: "Pascal's argument, instead of being powerless, then seems a regular clincher, and is the last stroke needed to make our faith in masses and holy water complete." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 11", causes: {from: pascal, to: belief, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: psychology, jointly: [pretend]}}

[A use for telepathy might have opened the evidence]: "if this very man had been shown something which as a scientist he might do with telepathy, he might not only have examined the evidence, but even have found it good enough." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 10", causes: {from: use, to: belief, sign: "+", hedged: true, basis: example, scope: singular, chain: psychology}}

# Absolutism and empiricism about truth (sections V-VI) {isGroup: true}

<No objective certitude>: Objective evidence and certitude are ideals never actually found, so the empiricist has no infallible signal to wait for before believing. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 12-17"}

(1) [Every supposed certainty has been doubted]: Apart from abstract propositions of comparison, "we find no proposition ever regarded by any one as evidently certain that has not either been called a falsehood, or at least had its truth sincerely questioned by some one else." {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 15"}
(2) [No test of truth has been agreed]: "No concrete test of what is really true has ever been agreed upon." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 15"}
(3) [Contradictory opinions have all claimed objective evidence]: "there is indeed nothing which some one has not thought absolutely true, while his neighbor deemed it absolutely false" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 16"}
-- {uses: [1, 2, 3]} --
(4) [Objective evidence is a mere aspiration]: The "lauded objective evidence is never triumphantly there, it is a mere aspiration or Grenzbegriff, marking the infinitely remote ideal of our thinking life." {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 16"}
(5) [The absolutist doctrine of objective evidence]: The absolutists hold that "we not only can attain to knowing truth, but we can know when we have attained to knowing it"; scholastic orthodoxy elaborates this as the doctrine of 'objective evidence'. #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 12-13", about: {way: absolutist, attain: yes, knowwhen: yes}}
(6) [Giving up certitude is not giving up truth]: "when as empiricists we give up the doctrine of objective certitude, we do not thereby give up the quest or hope of truth itself." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 17", note: "For the empiricist 'Not where it comes from but what it leads to is to decide': a hypothesis is true if 'the total drift of thinking continues to confirm it'."}
-- {uses: [4, 5, 6]} --
(7) [Empiricists have no bell to wait for]: "if we are empiricists, if we believe that no bell in us tolls to let us know for certain when truth is in our grasp, then it seems a piece of idle fantasticality to preach so solemnly our duty of waiting for the bell." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 30", about: {way: empiricist, knowwhen: no}}
    +> [Respect one another's mental freedom]

[Empiricists have no bell to wait for]
    <+ [Empiricists cannot infallibly know when they have truth]: "the empiricists think that although we may attain it, we cannot infallibly know when"
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 12", about: {way: empiricist, attain: yes, knowwhen: no}, note: "Section V's definition, set against the absolutists, who say 'we not only can attain to knowing truth, but we can know when we have attained to knowing it'. It is what the closing conditional's 'if we are empiricists' means; James declares himself 'a complete empiricist' on p. 15."}

[Only thus can opinions grow more true]: "I live, to be sure, by the practical faith that we must go on experiencing and thinking over our experience, for only thus can our opinions grow more true; but to hold any one of them---I absolutely do not care which---as if it never could be reinterpretable or corrigible, I believe to be a tremendously mistaken attitude" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 15", causes: {from: inquiry, to: truth, sign: "+", necessary: true, basis: asserted, chain: risk}}

[Certitude's application was the Inquisition]: "the most striking practical application to life of the doctrine of objective certitude has been the conscientious labors of the Holy Office of the Inquisition" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 16", causes: {from: certitude, to: inquisition, sign: "+", basis: example, chain: republic}}

# Two commandments (section VII) {isGroup: true}

<Believe truth and shun error are separable laws>: The evidentialist's preference for avoiding error over gaining truth is itself a passional choice, and not the only reasonable one. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 17-19"}

(1) [Two separable laws]: "We must know the truth; and we must avoid error,---these are our first and great commandments as would-be knowers; but they are not two ways of stating an identical commandment, they are two separable laws." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 17"}
(2) [Clifford chooses shunning error]: Clifford "exhorts us to the latter course. Believe nothing, he tells us, keep your mind in suspense forever, rather than by closing it on insufficient evidence incur the awful risk of believing lies." #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 18", about: {stance: wait, paramount: error}}
(3) [Duties about truth and error are passional]: "these feelings of our duty about either truth or error are in any case only expressions of our passional life." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 18", about: [{stance: wait, passional: yes}, {stance: believe, passional: yes}]}
-- {uses: [1, 2, 3]} --
(4) [Clifford's rule expresses a private horror of dupery]: he who says "Better go without belief forever than believe a lie!" "merely shows his own preponderant private horror of becoming a dupe." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 18", causes: {from: horror, to: veto, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}
(5) [Worse things than being duped can happen]: James also has "a horror of being duped; but I can believe that worse things than being duped may happen to a man in this world" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 19", note: "The general who keeps his soldiers out of battle forever rather than risk a single wound: 'Not so are victories either over enemies or over nature gained.'"}
-- {uses: [4, 5]} --
(6) [Clifford's exhortation is fantastic]: "Clifford's exhortation has to my ears a thoroughly fantastic sound." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 19"}
    -> [Clifford's rule]

[Escaping error does not bring truth]: "it hardly ever happens that by merely disbelieving B we necessarily believe A. We may in escaping B fall into believing other falsehoods, C or D, just as bad as B; or we may escape B by not believing anything at all, not even A." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 17", causes: {from: suspend, to: truth, sign: "-", basis: asserted, chain: risk}}

[Believing risks error for the chance of truth]: "You, on the other hand, may think that the risk of being in error is a very small matter when compared with the blessings of real knowledge, and be ready to be duped many times in your investigation rather than postpone indefinitely the chance of guessing true." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 18", note: "Put to the hearer as the other reasonable weighting; James then says he finds it impossible to go with Clifford.", causes: [{from: decide, to: truth, sign: "+", on: chance, basis: asserted, chain: risk}, {from: decide, to: error, sign: "+", on: chance, basis: asserted, chain: risk}]}

[Avoiding every risk wins no victories]: "It is like a general informing his soldiers that it is better to keep out of battle forever than to risk a single wound. Not so are victories either over enemies or over nature gained." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 19", causes: {from: norisk, to: victory, sign: "-", basis: example, design: illustration, chain: risk}}

# Where faith creates fact (section IX) {isGroup: true}

<Moral questions cannot wait for proof>: Moral questions are questions of worth, not of what sensibly exists, and only the heart can decide them. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 22-23"}

(1) [Moral questions concern worth, not existence]: "A moral question is a question not of what sensibly exists, but of what is good, or would be good if it did exist." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 22"}
(2) [Science cannot compare worths]: "Science can tell us what exists; but to compare the worths, both of what exists and of what does not exist, we must consult not science, but what Pascal calls our heart." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 22"}
-----
(3) [Moral belief is decided by the will]: "The question of having moral beliefs at all or not having them is decided by" our will; "Moral scepticism can no more be refuted or proved by logic than intellectual scepticism can." {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 22-23", note: "Joins two passages about a page apart: the opening sentence on p. 22-23 and the conclusion of the paragraph on p. 23.", causes: {from: willing, to: moralbelief, sign: which, basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}

<Faith can create its own facts>: In truths dependent on our personal action, a preliminary faith is often what brings the fact about, so a veto on faith running ahead of evidence would be insane. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 23-25"}

(1) [Personal relations depend on precursive faith]: "Whether you do or not depends, in countless instances, on whether I meet you half-way, am willing to assume that you must like me, and show you trust and expectation." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 23", note: "'You do or not' answers the example question 'Do you like me or not?'", causes: {from: faith, to: liking, sign: "+", necessary: true, basis: example, scope: general, chain: creation, given: ["in countless instances"]}}
(2) [Social organisms rest on mutual trust]: "Wherever a desired result is achieved by the co-operation of many independent persons, its existence as a fact is a pure consequence of the precursive faith in one another of those immediately concerned." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 24", note: "Governments, armies, ships, colleges, athletic teams; the train looted by a few highwaymen who can count on one another.", causes: {from: mutualtrust, to: result, sign: "+", necessary: true, basis: example, scope: general, chain: creation}}
-- {uses: [1, 2]} --
(3) [Some facts need a preliminary faith]: "There are, then, cases where a fact cannot come at all unless a preliminary faith exists in its coming." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 25", causes: {from: prefaith, to: fact, sign: "+", necessary: true, basis: example, scope: general, chain: creation}}
(4) [Where faith helps create the fact, forbidding it is insane]: "And where faith in a fact can help create the fact, that would be an insane logic which should say that faith running ahead of scientific evidence is the 'lowest kind of immorality' into which a thinking being can fall." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 25", causes: {from: prefaith, to: fact, sign: "+", sufficient: false, basis: asserted, scope: general, chain: creation}}
    -> [Clifford's rule]
-- {uses: [3, 4]} --
(5) [Faith based on desire is lawful in truths dependent on action]: "In truths dependent on our personal action, then, faith based on desire is certainly a lawful and possibly an indispensable thing." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 25"}

[The heart decides moral belief]: "If your heart does not want a world of moral reality, your head will assuredly never make you believe in one." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 23", causes: {from: willing, to: moralbelief, sign: "+", necessary: true, basis: asserted, chain: psychology}}

[Faith makes the liking come]: "The previous faith on my part in your liking's existence is in such cases what makes your liking come." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 23", causes: {from: faith, to: liking, sign: "+", on: being, basis: example, chain: creation}}

[Aloofness keeps the liking away]: "if I stand aloof, and refuse to budge an inch until I have objective evidence" "ten to one your liking never comes." {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 23", causes: {from: aloof, to: liking, sign: "-", on: chance, basis: example, chain: creation}}

